title: Why it is awkward
pid: 36d7a778-c7e0-4928-b462-9f1aa1630735
body: |2-
  - One layer at a time: each fork follows only its own children, and forks below do not report to the fork above.
  - Text is not followed. Once forked, one's text and the author's diverge silently.
  - Nothing records which version one forked from, so there is no three-way merge, and "has the author changed this since I took it?" has no answer.
  - Forking to answer or to take part replaces the author's note in one's own views (Ed: "I no longer see his note, only mine"), and leaving out one child forks the whole note.
  - Taking a change and acknowledging it are one act, containing it. There is nowhere to say "seen".
  - The author is not told. The author can see who subscribes or overrides (the subscriber and overrider folders), not what they did with the changes.
  Jeff's [[id:a7e61817-1ae8-4d95-a15f-f56ee1c825e5][the merge/update/subscribe procedure]] sketches a policy for
  merging whole graphs (prefer a side, take new nodes, resolve text by
  hand). It is about skgrepos, not subscriptions, but its question of
  what "vanished" means is the same baseline question.
